---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import { BREW_COMMAND, RELEASES_URL } from "../../lib/github";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<DocsLayout
  currentSlug="installation"
  title="Installation"
  description="Every package for every platform, and Standard vs Full."
>
  <p>
    Everything is on <a href={RELEASES_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub Releases</a>. The
    <a href={`${base}/#downloads`}>downloads list</a> links each file with its size and sha256, and
    <a href={`${base}/#pick`}>Help me pick</a> narrows it down to one. Swap <code>&lt;version&gt;</code> below for the
    release you have.
  </p>

  <h2 id="editions">Standard or Full</h2>
  <p>
    Every package comes in two editions. They're the same app; the only difference is the tree-sitter grammars used for
    syntax highlighting.
  </p>
  <p>
    Tree-sitter highlighting is off by default (flag <code>105-tree-sitter-highlighting</code>, on in the Max preset).
    Without it you get GitHub Desktop's highlighting, which both editions have built in. Turn it on in Standard and
    Settings › Advanced downloads the grammars, 300+ languages' worth. Full (<code>Corvene-Full-…</code>,
    <code>corvene-full</code> for .deb and .rpm) already has them, so it never needs to download anything. That's the one
    for machines without internet access. It's about 170 MB bigger once installed.
  </p>
  <p>Full updates to the next Full release.</p>

  <h2 id="macos">macOS</h2>
  <p>
    macOS 10.15.7 or later on Intel, 11 or later on Apple Silicon. You also need <code>git</code> 2.38 or later: the
    Command Line Tools from Xcode 14.3 on have it (<code>xcode-select --install</code>), or <code>brew install git</code>.
    Adding to an existing stash needs 2.40.
  </p>
  <h3>Homebrew</h3>
  <CodeBlock code={BREW_COMMAND} />
  <p>The easiest way. Homebrew clears the quarantine flag, so macOS doesn't stop the first launch.</p>
  <h3>Download</h3>
  <p>
    The <code>universal</code> .dmg runs on any Mac. The <code>arm64</code> (Apple Silicon) and <code>x86_64</code> (Intel)
    ones are smaller. The .zip files hold the same app.
  </p>
  <p>
    Corvene isn't notarized by Apple, so macOS blocks the first launch of a downloaded copy. Click Done, then go to System
    Settings › Privacy &amp; Security and click Open Anyway. On macOS 14 and older, right-click the app and choose Open
    instead. Or from Terminal:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code="xattr -d com.apple.quarantine /Applications/Corvene.app" />
  <p>You only do this once. Updates install without asking.</p>

  <h2 id="windows">Windows</h2>
  <p>
    Windows 10 or 11, on x64, ARM64 or 32-bit. The packages aren't code-signed yet, so SmartScreen warns the first time:
    click More info, then Run anyway. With Smart App Control turned on, Windows won't run them at all.
  </p>
  <h3>Installer</h3>
  <p>
    <code>Corvene-&lt;version&gt;-x86_64-setup.exe</code> (or <code>aarch64</code>, <code>i686</code>). Installs for your
    user only, no admin rights needed. It adds Corvene to the Start menu and <code>corvene</code> to your
    <code>PATH</code>, and keeps itself up to date. If there's no Git on the PC it offers to download one for Corvene.
  </p>
  <h3>.msi</h3>
  <p>
    Installs for every user into Program Files, for deploying with Group Policy or Intune. It doesn't update itself;
    deploy the next .msi instead. Needs <a href="https://git-scm.com/download/win" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Git for Windows</a>.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code="msiexec /i Corvene-<version>-x86_64.msi /qn" />
  <h3>Portable</h3>
  <p>
    Unzip <code>Corvene-&lt;version&gt;-windows-x86_64-portable.zip</code> anywhere, a USB stick included, and run
    <code>Corvene\corvene.exe</code>. Nothing gets installed and it doesn't update itself. Needs Git for Windows.
  </p>

  <h2 id="linux">Linux</h2>
  <p>
    Any distribution with glibc 2.35 or later (Ubuntu 22.04, Debian 12, Fedora 36 and newer), X11 or Wayland, and a
    Vulkan driver. For signed-in accounts you need a keyring: GNOME Keyring, KWallet or KeePassXC. And <code>git</code>
    2.38 or later, which Ubuntu 22.04 doesn't ship (use the git-core PPA there).
  </p>
  <p>
    Builds exist for x86_64 and arm64, plus 32-bit i686 and armhf that have had very little testing. Only the AppImage
    updates itself; for everything else, install the newer package.
  </p>

  <h3>Homebrew</h3>
  <CodeBlock code={BREW_COMMAND} />
  <p>Installs the AppImage as <code>~/Applications/Corvene.AppImage</code>. <code>brew upgrade</code> updates it.</p>

  <h3>Debian, Ubuntu, Mint</h3>
  <CodeBlock code="sudo apt install ./corvene_<version>_amd64.deb" />

  <h3>Fedora, RHEL, openSUSE</h3>
  <CodeBlock code="sudo dnf install ./corvene-<version>-1.x86_64.rpm" />
  <p>Or <code>sudo zypper install</code> on openSUSE.</p>

  <h3>AppImage</h3>
  <CodeBlock code={"chmod +x Corvene-<version>-x86_64.AppImage\n./Corvene-<version>-x86_64.AppImage"} />
  <p>The first run adds it to your app menu. It updates itself in place.</p>

  <h3>Flatpak</h3>
  <CodeBlock code="flatpak install --user Corvene-<version>-x86_64.flatpak" />
  <p>
    Comes with its own git and sees your home folder. To give it another folder:
    <code>flatpak override --user --filesystem=&lt;path&gt; com.wasimaster.corvene</code>.
  </p>

  <h3>Snap</h3>
  <CodeBlock code={"sudo snap install --dangerous Corvene-<version>-x86_64.snap\nsudo snap connect corvene:password-manager-service\nsudo snap connect corvene:ssh-keys"} />
  <p>Comes with git, Git LFS and OpenSSH. The two connects are for signing in and for git over SSH.</p>

  <h3>Tarball and Arch</h3>
  <p>
    <code>Corvene-&lt;version&gt;-linux-x86_64.tar.gz</code> has the same files as the .deb. Run
    <code>usr/lib/corvene/corvene</code> from where you unpacked it. On Arch, the <code>corvene-bin</code> and
    <code>corvene-full-bin</code> PKGBUILDs install this tarball.
  </p>

  <h2 id="android">Android</h2>
  <p>Android 8.0 or later. git, OpenSSH and Git LFS come inside the app. There are two apps, and they install side by side:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Corvene</strong> is new and made for phones. Still in progress.
      <code>Corvene-&lt;version&gt;-android-foss-arm64.apk</code>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Corvene Legacy</strong> is the desktop app itself, for tablets, Chromebooks and desktop mode.
      <code>Corvene-Legacy-&lt;version&gt;-android-foss-arm64.apk</code>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <code>arm64</code> fits nearly every device. There's also <code>armv7</code> for old 32-bit phones,
    <code>x86_64</code> for Intel and AMD Chromebooks, and <code>universal</code> if you're not sure. Open the APK on the
    device; Android asks once whether your browser may install apps. To update, install the newer APK over it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <code>play</code> APKs are the Google Play version, without "All files access". Full APKs come one per
    architecture, no universal one.
  </p>
  <p>Android support is experimental and has been tried on only a few devices.</p>
</DocsLayout>
